    "result_text": "All 292 tests pass. The change is complete.\n\n## Summary\n\nWhen creating an owner via `POST /api/owners`, the response now includes a `locality` field set to `'local'` if the owner's city is the single most common city among existing owners at that moment, otherwise `'remote'`.\n\nChanges made, following the established pattern of the other computed-at-creation fields (namesakeCount, sharesHousehold, etc.):\n\n- **`OwnerRestControllerV1.java`** \u2014 added `determineLocality(city)`, called in `addOwner` before persisting. It groups existing owners by city (case-insensitive), finds the max count, and returns `'local'` only when there is a *single* city holding that max and it matches the new owner's city; otherwise `'remote'`. No existing owners or a tie for most-common both yield `'remote'`.\n- **`Owner.java`** \u2014 added a `locality` field with getter/setter and a `@Column(name = \"locality\")` mapping.\n- **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added the read-only `locality` property to the `Owner` schema (the DTO is generated from this, and MapStruct maps it by name automatically).\n- **schema.sql** for all four databases (h2, hsqldb, postgres, mysql) \u2014 added the `locality VARCHAR(20)` column.\n- **`OwnerRestControllerV1Tests.java`** \u2014 added two tests: creating an owner in Madison (the seed data's single most-common city) returns `'local'`; creating one in a different city returns `'remote'`.\n\nNote the semantics: \"single most common\" means a unique maximum \u2014 if two cities are tied for the top count, no owner is considered `'local'`.",
